The `==` operator in Java is used to compare references (memory addresses) of objects, including strings. It returns true if the references are identical, pointing to the same object in memory. On the other hand, the `.equals()` method is used to compare the actual values (content) of objects, including strings. Here's when to use each comparison operator:

1. `==`: Use `==` when you want to compare two string references and check if they are the same object or if one variable was reassigned to the other. For example, to check if two string variables reference the same string object:
   ```java
   String str1 = "Some Text";
   String str2 = str1;
   if (str1 == str2) {
       System.out.println("str1 and str2 reference the same string object.");
   }
   ```
1. `.equals()`: Use the `.equals()` method when you want to compare the actual values of two string objects (or any object strings contain). This method handles cases where the strings have the same content but are stored in different memory locations:
   ```java
   String str3 = "Some Text";
   String str4 = new String("Some Text");
   if (str3.equals(str4)) {
       System.out.println("str3 and str4 have the same value.");
   }
   ```

In summary, `==` checks for reference equality, while `.equals()` checks for value equality. Using `==` on string objects can lead to unexpected results if the strings contain different values but reference the same object. Therefore, it's recommended to use `.equals()` for string comparisons in most cases.

